Chelsea entered the competition in the third round, as one of the eight teams from the [[Premier League]] involved in European competition during the season.<ref>{{cite news |title=Carling Cup first round draw made |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/5098588.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=21 June 2006 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref> They were drawn against [[Blackburn Rovers F.C.|Blackburn Rovers]]; the match took place at [[Ewood Park]]. A goal apiece from [[Joe Cole]] and [[Salomon Kalou]] ensured a 2–0 victory.<ref>{{cite news |title=Blackburn 0–2 Chelsea |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6072594.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=25 October 2006 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref>

Chelsea's opponent in the fourth round were [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]]. The match was played at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] on 8 November 2006. [[Frank Lampard]] opened the scoring for Chelsea in the 32nd minute; further goals by [[Andriy Shevchenko]], [[Michael Essien]] and [[Didier Drogba]] in the second half secured a 4–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea 4–0 Aston Villa |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6116396.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=8 November 2006 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref>

[[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] hosted Chelsea at [[St James' Park]] in the fifth round of the competition. The home team came close to scoring after the midway point of the first half; [[Obafemi Martins]]'s shot towards the Chelsea goal hit the underside of the crossbar and bounced down, before the ball was cleared. In the 79th minute, Drogba – on as a substitute – scored the winning goal of the match, from a free-kick.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea underscore the fine line between success and failure again |first=George |last=Caulkin |page=84 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=21 December 2006}}</ref>

[[Wycombe Wanderers F.C.|Wycombe Wanderers]] were Chelsea's opponent in the semi-final which was played over [[Two-legged tie|two-legs]]. The first leg was staged at Wycombe's home ground, [[Adams Park]]. A match preview in ''[[The Guardian]]'' noted the financial gap between the two sides: "Wycombe's most expensive player, [[Jermaine Easter]], cost £80,000; Chelsea's squad is valued at more than £300m."<ref>{{cite news |title=Wycombe won't dare to bare in cup upset |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/jan/10/newsstory.wycombe |first=Stuart |last=James |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=10 January 2007 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref> In the game Wycombe started brightly, but the team were unable to convert their chances. Chelsea took the lead in the 36th minute when [[Wayne Bridge]] collected a pass from Kalou and chipped the ball over [[Ricardo Batista (footballer)|Ricardo Batista]], the Wycombe goalkeeper. Easter equalised for Wycombe with 13 minutes of the match remaining; neither side scored again and the match ended 1–1.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wycombe 1–1 Chelsea |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6236711.stm |first=Chris |last=Bevan |work=BBC Sport |date=10 January 2007 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref> The second leg at Stamford Bridge saw Chelsea start better of the two sides and two goals apiece from Shevchenko and Lampard meant they won 4–0 on the night, 5–1 on aggregate score to progress to the final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Shevchenko finds touch to set up all-London final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/jan/24/match.chelsea |first=Simon |last=Burnton |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=24 January 2007 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref>

