Arsenal entered the competition in the third round as they participated in European football during the season. They faced [[West Bromwich Albion F.C.|West Bromwich Albion]] at 
[[The Hawthorns]], where striker [[Jérémie Aliadière]] scored twice to secure a 2–0 victory for the visitors.<ref>{{cite news |title=West Brom 0–2 Arsenal |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/6072672.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=24 October 2006 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref>

Arsenal travelled to [[Goodison Park]] in the fourth round of the competition to play [[Everton F.C.|Everton]]. They won the match 1–0 courtesy of a late [[Emmanuel Adebayor]] goal, which came from a corner. For much of the game Everton played with a man disadvantage as striker [[James McFadden]] was sent off in the 19th minute for dissent.<ref>{{cite news |title=Everton 0–1 Arsenal |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/6116408.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=8 November 2006 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref>

[[File:Arsenal-Tottenham Carling Cup Second Leg End.ogv|left|thumb|Closing stages of Arsenal's second leg match against Tottenham Hotspur at the Emirates Stadium.]]
[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] were Arsenal's opponent in the fifth round. The match, scheduled on 19 December 2006 at [[Anfield]], was postponed by referee [[Martin Atkinson]] because of heavy fog.<ref>{{cite news |title=Benitez & Wenger rue postponement |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6195047.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=19 December 2006 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref> Atkinson's decision infuriated the managers of both clubs, with [[Rafael Benítez]] commenting: "There were a lot of people looking forward to the game and it's really difficult to explain."<ref>{{cite news |title=Chaos looms through Anfield fog |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/2352625/Chaos-looms-through-Anfield-fog.html |first=Tim |last=Rich |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=20 December 2006 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref> The tie was rescheduled for 9 January 2007 and on the night Arsenal took the lead when Aliadière scored in the 27th minute. [[Robbie Fowler]] equalised for Liverpool six minutes later. Later, two goals from [[Júlio Baptista]] and goal from [[Alex Song]] put Arsenal 4–1 ahead at half time. In the second half, Baptista completed his hat-trick; although [[Steven Gerrard]] and [[Sami Hyypiä]] scored to close the scoreline gap for Liverpool, Baptista added his fourth goal of the match in the 84th minute. The final score was 6–3, Liverpool's heaviest defeat at Anfield in 76 years.<ref>{{cite news |title=Baptista revels in Arsenal's extravaganza |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2305855/Baptista-revels-in-Arsenals-extravaganza.html |first=Henry |last=Winter |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=10 January 2007 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref> In his match report for ''The Guardian'', Daniel Taylor praised Arsenal's reserve team and summarised: "The difference between the two teams was immense. Arsenal played with flair and purpose; Liverpool were dishevelled and short of leadership."<ref>{{cite news |title=Baptista grabs four as Liverpool hit for six |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/jan/10/match.arsenal |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=10 January 2007 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref>

[[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] faced Arsenal in the semi-final which was played over two-legs. The first leg was at Tottenham's home ground, [[White Hart Lane]]. A goal from [[Dimitar Berbatov]] gave Tottenham the lead in the 12th minute and they extended their advantage after Baptista inadvertently kicked the ball into his own goal. Baptista, however, made amends in the second half, scoring twice in the space of 13 minutes to level the score at 2–2.<ref>{{cite news |title=Baptista at the double spikes Spurs |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/jan/25/match.arsenal |first=Kevin |last=McCarra |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=25 January 2007 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref> The second leg at the [[Emirates Stadium]] saw Arsenal take the lead in the 77th minute when Adebayor scored. [[Mido (footballer)|Mido]] equalised for Tottenham, which took the match into extra time because the [[away goals rule]] was not in use in the competition. Aliadière's goal in the 105th minute restored Arsenal's lead and an own goal scored by [[Pascal Chimbonda]] ensured the home team progressed to the final, winning 3–1 after extra time, and 5–3 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arsenal 3–1 Tottenham (agg 5–3) |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6305313.stm |first=Sam |last=Lyon |work=BBC Sport |date=31 January 2007 |access-date=5 July 2014}}</ref>

==Build-up==
Chelsea, the match favourites,<ref name=clock>{{cite news |title=Carling Cup final clockwatch |first=Saj |last=Chowdhury |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6392761.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=25 February 2007 |access-date=25 February 2007}}</ref> were appearing in their fifth League Cup final; they had previously won the competition three times in [[1965 Football League Cup final|1965]], [[1998 Football League Cup final|1998]] and [[2005 Football League Cup final|2005]], and lost one final in [[1972 Football League Cup final|1972]]. By contrast Arsenal made their sixth League Cup final appearance; they had won two in [[1987 Football League Cup final|1987]] and [[1993 Football League Cup final|1993]] and lost three in [[1965 Football League Cup final|1968]], [[1969 Football League Cup final|1969]] and [[1988 Football League Cup final|1988]].<ref>{{cite web|first=James |last=Ross |title=Football League Cup Finals |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/engleagcuphist.html |publisher=Rec. Sport. Soccer Statistics Foundation (RSSSF) |date=20 March 2014 |access-date=6 July 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160329230552/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/engleagcuphist.html |archive-date=29 March 2016}}</ref> Both clubs had played each other in the League Cup four times, with Chelsea and Arsenal each winning on two occasions. Chelsea's biggest win against Arsenal in the competition was a 5–0 away win in November 1998.{{sfn|The Football League|p=6}} Prior to the match Arsenal's last win against Chelsea in all competitions came in February 2004.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea v Arsenal (Sun) |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6371613.stm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070225093808/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/league_cup/6371613.stm |archive-date=25 February 2007 |work=BBC Sport |date=23 February 2007 |url-status=live |access-date=2 December 2014}}</ref>

The relationship between both clubs had become strained, not least because of Chelsea's financial advantage; the club was owned by billionaire [[Roman Abramovich]]. The day after Chelsea announced losses of £80&nbsp;million for 2005–06, Arsenal manager [[Arsène Wenger]] expressed concern over his opponent's conduct: "I have said many times that all the clubs should be balanced with their natural resources. That means they can only spend what they earn."<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger says champions should not profit from losses |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/20/newsstory.sport6 |first=David |last=Ornstein |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=20 February 2007 |access-date=6 July 2014}}</ref> Chelsea manager [[José Mourinho]] refused to respond to Wenger's comments in his [[UEFA Champions League]] pre-match press conference, but made reference to his record in the competition in contrast to his counterpart: "Many great managers in the world have never won it. The big example is not far from us; Arsène Wenger is a top manager in the world. He's a big manager in the world of football and he never did it so when I did it once, I can thank God for that. I have that."<ref>{{cite news |title=Mourinho turns up heat with Wenger dig |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2308081/Mourinho-turns-up-heat-with-Wenger-dig.html |first=John |last=Ley |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=21 February 2007 |access-date=6 July 2014}}</ref>

{{Quote box|width=320px|bgcolor=#FFFFF0 |align=right|quote="You have singers in England going on ''The X Factor'' where talents are discovered which nobody knew about. I like it because people are brave enough to come out and they want to achieve something in their life. They are ready to be committed to do it. They have a target. People who turn up and are ready to fight for something, like our young players are, I like. It's the ''X Factor'' Academy!"|salign = left |source=[[Arsène Wenger]], February 2007<ref name="wenpr">{{cite news |title=Wenger enters great debate |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2308170/Wenger-enters-great-debate.html |first=John |last=Ley |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=23 February 2007 |access-date=2 March 2014}}</ref>}}

Wenger replied to Mourinho's comments by arguing that although plenty of managers have won the Champions League, most would not be considered greats – "What is important is that you look at their careers in 10, 15 or 20 years. What kind of quality have you brought through in your work and on how consistent a level have you done it?"<ref name="gudr">{{cite news |title=Nothing special about Mourinho, says Wenger |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/23/newsstory.sport3 |first=David |last=Ornstein |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=23 February 2007 |access-date=2 March 2014}}</ref>

Much was made of Arsenal's policy of using the League Cup to field youngsters. In order to explain his team selection, Wenger made an analogy between his youth players and hopefuls on ''[[The X Factor (British TV series)|The X Factor]]'', a television programme he admired.<ref name="wenpr"/> He ruled out playing his more experienced players for the match: "We battled very hard to be in this final and it would be a reward for this team, which has risen up to every challenge."<ref name="gudr"/> Mourinho did not anticipate Arsenal would make a "crazy change" to their team: "Normally, they change players but keep their philosophy, their idea of football, their system."<ref>{{cite news |title=Hot Gunners certain to be on fire – Jose |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Hot+Gunners+certain+to+be+on+fire+-+Jose.-a0159758337 |first=Harry |last=Noble |newspaper=Sunday Mercury |location=Birmingham |date=25 February 2007 |access-date=6 July 2014}}</ref> He believed the final had the makings of a showpiece event as the competition was not a main target for both clubs; emotions therefore would not dictate matters.<ref>{{cite web |title=Mourinho: It could be a cup final classic |url=http://www.espnfc.com/story/410352/mourinho-predicts-classic-carling-cup-final |work=ESPN FC |date=23 February 2007 |access-date=6 July 2014}}</ref>

Both clubs received an allocation of approximately 30,000 tickets. The competition sponsor [[Carling]] gave up its allocation; they instead invited 200 fans of Arsenal and Chelsea to be seated in their suite.<ref>{{cite news |title=Intoxicating mix hits the spot for clubs and sponsor |first=Nick |last=Szczepanik |page=91 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=27 February 2007}}</ref> Supporters of the four semi-final clubs were invited to participate in the "Carling Cabinet"; this gave them the opportunity to have their say about the matchday experience.{{sfn|The Football League|p=3}} Ticket prices for adults were priced £36, £48, £64 and £74, with concessions for juniors and seniors.<ref>{{cite web |title=Carling Cup final tickets and travel |url=http://forums.cfcnet.co.uk/topic/23150-carling-cup-final-ticket-details/ |publisher=CFCnet |date=25 January 2007 |access-date=2 December 2014 |archive-date=4 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304064555/http://forums.cfcnet.co.uk/topic/23150-carling-cup-final-ticket-details/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> For every ticket sold, the Football League gave [[Pound sterling|£]]1 to [[Children in Need]]; the donation came at a total of £70,000.{{sfn|The Football League|p=3}}

Chelsea were expected to line up in a [[Formation (association football)#4–4–2|4–4–2]] formation, with Essien in central defence.{{sfn|The Football League|p=2}} They had doubts about the fitness of captain [[John Terry]] going into the final. He appeared to have damaged his ankle ligaments during the first leg of Chelsea's Champions League tie with [[FC Porto|Porto]], though passed a fitness test just days after.<ref>{{cite news |title=Terry back for Carling Cup final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/chelsea/6384683.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=24 February 2007 |access-date=6 July 2014}}</ref> Arsenal were expected to line up in a similar formation, with Baptista and Aliadière up front.{{sfn|The Football League|p=2}}

Train delays caused by a "major signal failure" meant that on the day of the final over 2,000 spectators were late for kick-off.<ref>{{cite news |title=Train problems hold up 2,000 fans |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/wales/6394985.stm |work=BBC News |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=26 February 2007}}</ref> An accident on the [[M4 motorway]] trigged more chaos; this led to requests for the game to be delayed, but [[South Wales Police]] were confident they could get everyone seated in time.<ref name=clock/> [[Network Rail]] later apologised for the delay.<ref>{{cite news |title=Apology for football fans' delays |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/wales/6394985.stm |work=BBC News |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=26 February 2007}}</ref>

==Match==
Chelsea set up in a [[Formation (association football)#4.E2.80.931.E2.80.933.E2.80.932|4–1–3–2]] formation with Terry partnering [[Ricardo Carvalho]] in defence. [[Claude Makélélé]] played behind a midfield three made up of Lampard, Ballack and Essien. By contrast Arsenal lined up in a 4–4–2 formation; [[Abou Diaby]] and [[Theo Walcott]] were positioned either side of central midfielders [[Cesc Fàbregas]] and [[Denílson Pereira Neves|Denílson]] and provided width. Arsenal's starting line up – averaging 21 years – was the youngest to play in a major English cup final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Black and blue |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Football%3A+BLACK+AND+BLUE%3B+CARLING+CUP+ARSENAL+1+CHELSEA+2+3+sent+off...-a0159774369 |first=Colin |last=Duncan |newspaper=Daily Record |location=Glasgow |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref><ref name="ave">{{cite news |title=Wenger able to stomach defeat after a display of prodigious promise |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/26/newsstory.sport |first=Dominic |last=Fifield |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref> Before the match commenced, the national anthem was sung by [[Juliette Pochin]].<ref name=clock/>

===Summary===
[[File:Chelsea Vs Arsenal - Carling Cup Final 25 Feb 2007.jpg|thumb|Pre-match presentation]]
Arsenal kicked off and started strongly, with Fàbregas dictating play in midfield.<ref name=clock/> Baptista came close to scoring in the seventh minute, but for his shot to be blocked by Terry.<ref name=clock/> Another shot of his three minutes later was saved by [[Petr Čech]], who tipped the ball over the crossbar. This resulted in an Arsenal corner, which Drogba cleared.<ref name=gucl>{{cite news |title=Minute-by-minute report |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/25/newsstory.sport |first=Paul |last=Doyle |work=The Guardian|date=25 February 2014 |access-date=6 July 2014}}</ref> Walcott immediately collected the ball, passed it to Diaby before receiving it once more in the Chelsea penalty box. He sidefooted the ball past Čech and into the Chelsea goal to give Arsenal a 1–0 lead; it was his first goal for the club.<ref name=gucl/><ref>{{cite news |title=Top Five: Theo Walcott moments |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/arsenal/5295670/Top-Five-Theo-Walcott-moments.html |first=Emily |last=Benammar |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=8 May 2009 |access-date=10 July 2014}}</ref> Arsenal's tempo and the speed in which they passed the ball continued to cause Chelsea problems. Walcott in the 14th minute used his pace to get the better of defenders Bridge and Carvalho and crossed the ball to Baptista, who lost his footing.<ref name=gucl/>

Chelsea created their first chance of the match after 18 minutes – a shot from Lampard which hit [[Kolo Touré]].<ref name=gucl/> They fought back to equalise two minutes later. Ballack launched the ball to Drogba on the right-hand side of Arsenal's penalty area. Diverting his markers who attempted to set the offside trap, Drogba took the ball and shot it past [[Manuel Almunia]]'s goal to score.<ref name=clock/> The equaliser led to Chelsea asserting themselves in the match as the team began to build up possession. Denílson, at the midway point of the half, received the game's first yellow card for a foul on Essien; the Chelsea midfielder was later booked for a tackle on Baptista.<ref name=gucl/> Sticks of celery were thrown at Fàbregas before he took a corner close to stoppage time; targeting opponents with the vegetable was a Chelsea supporter's ritual going back more than two decades and originated from song.<ref name=gucl/><ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea fans captured on canvas |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/chelsea/6667839.stm |first=John |last=Sinnott |work=BBC Sport |date=18 May 2007 |access-date=10 July 2014}}</ref>

Chelsea made an attacking substitution before the start of the second half; [[Arjen Robben]] came on for Makélélé.<ref name=clock/> He made an immediate impact, running past the Arsenal defence in the 48th minute, but his pass failed to reach Shevchenko.<ref name=clock/> Soon after Carvalho was booked for a foul on Aliadière and conceded a free-kick at the edge of Chelsea's penalty box, though nothing came out of it.<ref name=gucl/> Both teams continued to fashion chances – a well worked move by Arsenal finished with Fàbregas's shooting low past Čech in goal, while Chelsea's Robben from 20 yards curled the ball wide.<ref name=gucl/> [[Lassana Diarra]] was shown a yellow card in the 52nd minute for his challenge on Diaby; the Arsenal midfielder managed to get past Ballack and Lampard before he was "deliberately bowled over" by his opponent.<ref name=gucl/> Robben earned Chelsea a corner moments after; from it, Terry attempted a diving header and Diaby, in an attempt to clear, ended up kicking the Chelsea captain, thus knocking him out.<ref name=clock/> After receiving medical attention Terry was immediately stretchered off and taken to the hospital; he was replaced by [[Mikel John Obi]] and Essien was moved to centre back.<ref name=clock/>
[[File:Chelsea v Arsenal - Carling Cup - 25-02-07 (403745490).jpg|thumb|left|Fracas between the Arsenal and Chelsea players]]
The period immediately after Terry's substitution saw few clear-cut chances, with Walcott and Drogba barely testing their respective keepers.<ref name=gucl/> Arsenal made two substitutions in a three-minute spell – [[Armand Traoré]] for [[Emmanuel Eboué]] and [[Alexander Hleb]] replacing the injured Diaby. In the 74th minute a shot by Lampard from 25 yards hit Almunia's crossbar. Arsenal responded with a flowing move from left to right, which resulted in a corner. Chelsea cleared and countered, forcing Almunia to save.<ref name=gucl/> Arsenal made their final substitution in the 80th minute, replacing Aliadière with Adebayor.<ref name=gucl/> With six minutes remaining of the 90, Chelsea scored the winning goal of the match. Arsenal gave away possession to Essien in midfield, who passed the ball to Robben wide on the left. He crossed the ball into the Arsenal penalty area, which reached the head of Drogba; the striker headed it in past Almunia.<ref name=clock/>

Shevchenko came close to scoring Chelsea's third goal of the match, but his effort hit the crossbar.<ref name=clock/> Seven minutes of [[stoppage time]] were added to the match due to Terry's injury.<ref name=gucl/> A tussle between Touré and Mikel over shirt pulling resulted in a fracas between both sets of players. Both managers came onto the pitch in order to calm matters.<ref name=gucl/> Referee [[Howard Webb]] decided to send off Touré and Mikel for violent conduct and consulted with an assistant referee, before sending off Adebayor, who was alleged to have struck Bridge.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger: Adebayor is innocent |url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/11668/2406825/wenger-adebayor-is-innocent |publisher=Sky Sports |first=Phil |last=Jackson |date=3 March 2007 |access-date=10 July 2014}}</ref> Adebayor was irate and needed to be escorted off the field by Arsenal officials.<ref name=gucl/> The match restarted, by which point Kalou came on for Shevchenko.<ref name=gucl/>

No further goals were scored and the full-time whistle eventually blew in the 102nd minute of play.<ref>{{cite news |title=All the action blow by blow |first=Bill |last=Edgar |page=84 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=26 February 2007}}</ref> Terry who was admitted to hospital later discharged himself and returned to the stadium to celebrate with his teammates.<ref>{{cite news |title=Terry recovers after head injury |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/chelsea/6395301.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=25 February 2007 |access-date=25 February 2007}}</ref>

