==Post-match and aftermath==
[[File:Chelseawincarlingcup.jpg|thumb|The Chelsea players celebrating their victory]]
Chelsea's victory meant they won the trophy for the sixth time. Mourinho was delighted to have won the League Cup and felt his team deserved to win on balance of play. He credited his opponents, with a sly dig: "A special word for them – they played very good. They have a great coach, great players – but football is about winning and the cup goes to us."<ref>{{cite news |title=Mourinho praises Arsenal youngsters |url=http://www.breakingnews.ie/sport/soccer/mourinho-praises-arsenal-youngsters-299612.html |work=BreakingNews.ie |date=25 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref> He was disappointed by the fracas and pointed out it was born out of Arsenal's frustration, though stressed "We cannot kill a player because something happened."<ref>{{cite news |title=Mourinho and Wenger join the fray in final of three red cards |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/26/newsstory.carlingcup0607 |first=Matt |last=Scott |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref> Mourinho took time out to praise Arsenal's medical staff for attending to Terry after his concussion and absolved Diaby from any fault.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea's past scares made Mourinho fear for Terry |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/26/newsstory.sport1 |first=Matt |last=Scott |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref>

Lampard described his teammate Drogba as “...special, he's the best in the world at the moment. He does it week in, week out and on the big occasions." He argued the brawl near the end of the match highlighted how much the players from both sides wanted to win.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cup final brawl upsets Mourinho |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6394515.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=25 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref> Defender [[Ashley Cole]], who left Arsenal in acrimonious terms to join Chelsea felt his decision to move was vindicated: "I'm enjoying life, the manager's good and after six or seven months here I've already won a medal. That's why I came here."<ref>{{cite news |title=Cole: I feel a part of it now |url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/11668/2407074/cole-i-feel-a-part-of-it-now |publisher=Sky Sports |first=Carla |last=Hilton |date=3 March 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref>

Wenger apologised for his team's behaviour: "I feel we should not have reacted at all. Overall we are sorry for what happened. When you do not behave like you want to, then you have to apologise."<ref name="wen">{{cite news |title=Wenger says sorry for Arsenal's part in cup final brawl |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/28/newsstory.sport1 |first=Marc |last=Isaacs |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=28 February 2007 |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref> He had no regrets over his team selection and suggested it was part of the player's learning process.<ref name="ave"/> Wenger said of Walcott: "...he had a very good performance and scored a great goal. That is good for us and good news for England."<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger impressed with Walcott |url=http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/11670/2406492/wenger-impressed-with-walcott |publisher=Sky Sports |first=Alex |last=Livie |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref> [[Gilberto Silva]] described Arsenal's defeat as a "sad memory" and wanted to use the loss to motivate the team for their coming matches.<ref name="wen"/>

The day after the final Arsenal and Chelsea submitted claims for [[wrongful dismissal]] of Adebayor and Mikel, respectively, to [[The Football Association]] (FA).<ref>{{cite web |title=Wrongful dismissal claims |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFA/Disciplinary/NewsAndFeatures/Postings/2007/02/Claims26Feb.htm |archive-url=https://archive.today/20080621190841/http://www.thefa.com/TheFA/Disciplinary/NewsAndFeatures/Postings/2007/02/Claims26Feb.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 June 2008 |publisher=The Football Association |date=27 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014 }}</ref> The FA denied both appeals and clarified their penalties.<ref>{{cite web |title=Claims rejected |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFA/Disciplinary/NewsAndFeatures/Postings/2007/02/AdebayorMikelrejected.htm |archive-url=https://archive.today/20080514004038/http://www.thefa.com/TheFA/Disciplinary/NewsAndFeatures/Postings/2007/02/AdebayorMikelrejected.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 May 2008 |publisher=The Football Association |date=27 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014 }}</ref> Eboué was charged with violent conduct for striking Bridge, whereas Adebayor was charged for his behaviour after being sent off.<ref name="fasta">{{cite web |title=FA Statement |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFA/Disciplinary/NewsAndFeatures/Postings/2007/02/StatementCarlingCupFinal.htm |archive-url=https://archive.today/20080514004043/http://www.thefa.com/TheFA/Disciplinary/NewsAndFeatures/Postings/2007/02/StatementCarlingCupFinal.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 May 2008 |publisher=The Football Association |date=27 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014 }}</ref> Both players were suspended for three matches – one less than Mikel.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA rejects player appeals as Chelsea and Arsenal brace themselves |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/28/newsstory.arsenal |first=Matt |last=Scott |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref> Arsenal and Chelsea were charged with misconduct concerning their inability to control their players and were fined £100,000 each.<ref name="fasta"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Arsenal & Chelsea fined £100,000 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/league_cup/6500213.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=27 March 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref> Wenger criticised the FA's treatment of his players and accused the linesman of lying in his account of Adebayor's actions. This led to an investigation where Wenger was found guilty; he was later fined £2,500 and censured by the FA.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger fined and censured by FA |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/a/arsenal/6620609.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=3 May 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref>

{{Quote box|width=320px|bgcolor=#FFFFF0 |align=left|quote="Since I arrived, they have won nothing. Since we are at Chelsea, they lost Premierships, they lost the Charity Shield to us, they lost the Carling Cup to us. I don't think they are winning a lot. Absolutely brilliant young boys, manager top, team with a great future. But they didn't win."|salign = left|source=[[José Mourinho]] on Arsenal, February 2007<ref>{{cite news |title=Mourinho hits out at Ronaldo and 'lucky United' |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2308379/Mourinho-hits-out-at-Ronaldo-and-lucky-United.html |first=John |last=Ley |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=27 February 2007 |access-date=2 March 2014}}</ref>}}

Journalists reviewing the match praised Arsenal's young team; some however went as far to criticise their conduct in the brawl. [[Henry Winter]] wrote in ''[[The Daily Telegraph]]'' of 26 February 2007 "...all feline grace and youthful ambition, [Arsenal] were the better team, moving the ball around exquisitely at times,"<ref name="wint">{{cite news |title=Chelsea take control amid the carnage |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2308299/Chelsea-take-control-amid-the-carnage.html |first=Henry |last=Winter |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref> while [[Glenn Moore (journalist)|Glenn Moore]] of ''[[The Independent]]'' reflected "the Arsenal manager can take great consolation from the quality of his young footballers."<ref>{{cite news |title=Walcott dash of brilliance eclipsed as Drogba makes experience tell |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-10747306.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714221706/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-10747306.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=14 July 2014 |first=Glenn |last=Moore |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=8 July 2014}} </ref> ''The Guardian'' correspondent [[Kevin McCarra]] opened his match report with the line "To Chelsea the trophy, to Arsenal the future," and felt the latter club deserved plaudits for the role they played in the final, and competition as a whole. He commended Chelsea's adversity and singled out Carvalho as an example; at fault for Arsenal's goal, the defender's performance thereafter was faultless.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea snatch the cup that boils over |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/feb/26/match.arsenal |first=Kevin |last=McCarra |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref> [[Matt Dickinson (journalist)|Matt Dickinson]] of ''[[The Times]]'' agreed that Arsenal had played the better football, but said their temperament was "brittle" – "[They] had lost the trophy before they lost their heads."<ref>{{cite news |title=Tempers snap in the Snarling Cup |first=Matt |last=Dickinson |page=S2 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=26 February 2007}}</ref> Football pundit [[Alan Hansen]] was scathing of Arsenal's behaviour and suggested their inexperience had a part to play: "When they reflect upon it, Arsenal will admit that they were largely in the wrong."<ref name="hans">{{cite news |title=No excuse for losing the game and the plot |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/columnists/alanhansen/2308316/No-excuse-for-losing-the-game-and-the-plot.html |first=Alan |last=Hansen |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref> He was critical of Wenger's team selection and felt the big clubs saw the competition, much like the [[FA Cup]] as an irrelevance.<ref name="hans"/> The violence and sendings off on the pitch led to some media outlets dubbing the match as the "Snarling Cup final",<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/from-kanus-cracker-to-the-snarling-cup--the-top-five-arsenal-v-chelsea-matches-9016060.html?action=gallery&ino=1 |title= From Kanu's cracker to the 'Snarling Cup' – the top five Arsenal v Chelsea matches|newspaper=London Evening Standard |access-date=8 July 2014}}</ref> or in the case of Winter's match report the "Carnage Cup final".<ref name="wint"/>

This was the seventh and last League Cup final to be played at the [[Millennium Stadium]] in Cardiff, before the opening of the new [[Wembley Stadium]], the final's traditional venue.<ref>{{cite web |title=Stadium History |url=http://www.millenniumstadium.com/history/ |publisher=Millennium Stadium |access-date=8 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150531021502/http://www.millenniumstadium.com/history/ |archive-date=31 May 2015 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The match was broadcast live in the United Kingdom on [[Sky Sports|Sky Sports 1]]; the channel's coverage averaged at 2.7 million viewers, with a peak audience of 3.2 million (24.88%) at 5pm. At the time it was the biggest multichannel audience of 2007, overtaking a football match between Arsenal and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in January.<ref>{{cite news|title=Carling Cup grabs convincing win for Sky Sports |url=http://www.broadcastnow.co.uk/carling-cup-grabs-convincing-win-for-sky-sports/119680.article |work=Broadcast |first=Jon |last=Rogers |location=London |date=26 February 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}} {{subscription required}}</ref>

Chelsea's first match after the final was against [[Portsmouth F.C.|Portsmouth]] in the Premier League. A 2–0 win ensured they remained in contention for the league, though by May finished in second place.<ref>{{cite news |title=Drogba maintains Chelsea's chase |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/mar/04/match.sport |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |first=Amy |last=Lawrence |date=4 March 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/6603119.stm |title=How the title was won |work=BBC Sport |first=Paul |last=Fletcher |date=6 May 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref> Chelsea did however beat Manchester United to win the [[2007 FA Cup final]] and complete a cup double.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea 1–0 Man Utd |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/6649815.stm |first=Andrew |last=McKenzie |work=BBC Sport |date=3 May 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref> Arsenal played Blackburn Rovers a few days after the final in an FA Cup replay. They lost the match 1–0; it was Arsenal's third defeat in all competitions which represented their worst run of form in five years.<ref>{{cite news |title=McCarthy's late strike breaks Arsenal's hearts |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/mar/01/match.newsstory |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |date=1 March 2007 |access-date=9 July 2014}}</ref>

